  <!-- ANAMORPHISM — conformance Q&A (pattern phase → ΔR) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS AN ANAMORPHISM IN CATEGORY THEORY</pattern>
    <template>An anamorphism is the categorical dual of a catamorphism — where a catamorphism folds a structure down to a value, an anamorphism unfolds a seed value upward into a recursive data structure. It is defined by a coalgebra f: A → F A that describes how to expand one step at a time.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DOES AN ANAMORPHISM USE A COALGEBRA TO UNFOLD A SEED</pattern>
    <template>A coalgebra f: A → F A maps a seed value to one layer of structure. The anamorphism repeatedly applies f, building the full recursive structure layer by layer without storing it all at once — this is what makes it well-suited for corecursive (potentially infinite) structures like streams.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN ANAMORPHISM AND CATAMORPHISM</pattern>
    <template>A catamorphism (fold) consumes a recursive structure to produce a single value; an anamorphism (unfold) produces a recursive structure from a single seed value. They are categorical duals: catamorphism is an algebra morphism from the initial algebra, anamorphism is a coalgebra morphism to the terminal coalgebra.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- ANAMORPHISM — conformance Q&A (relation phase → ΔW) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS A HYLOMORPHISM AND HOW DOES IT COMBINE UNFOLD AND FOLD</pattern>
    <template>A hylomorphism is the composition of an anamorphism followed by a catamorphism: first unfold a seed into an intermediate structure, then fold that structure into a result. It represents any recursive algorithm that builds up and then tears down a structure — like merge sort (unfold into halves, fold by merging).</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DOES ANAMORPHISM RELATE TO CORECURSION IN TYPE THEORY</pattern>
    <template>Anamorphism formalizes corecursion: a corecursive definition produces structure incrementally from a seed without requiring the full output to be known in advance. In type theory, corecursive types (coinductive types) are exactly the types inhabited by anamorphisms — streams and infinite trees are canonical examples.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS A PARAMORPHISM IN RECURSION SCHEMES</pattern>
    <template>A paramorphism extends a catamorphism by giving the algebra access to both the recursive result AND the original sub-structure at each step. This lets you express operations like tails (which needs both the folded result and the remaining list) that a plain catamorphism cannot express directly.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS AN APOMORPHISM IN RECURSION SCHEMES</pattern>
    <template>An apomorphism extends an anamorphism by allowing the coalgebra to short-circuit at any step: instead of always producing a new seed to unfold further, it can return a fully-formed sub-structure directly. This makes it the dual of a paramorphism.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS A ZYGOMORPHISM IN RECURSION SCHEMES</pattern>
    <template>A zygomorphism is a catamorphism that uses a second auxiliary catamorphism to compute a helper value at each step. It allows mutual recursion to be expressed as a single structured recursion — the name comes from Greek "zygo" meaning paired or yoked.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- ANAMORPHISM — conformance Q&A (confidence phase → ΔC) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>IS AN ANAMORPHISM ALWAYS PRODUCTIVE FOR INFINITE STREAMS</pattern>
    <template>Productivity (guardedness) is not guaranteed automatically — the coalgebra must be guarded to ensure each recursive call is wrapped in at least one constructor. In total functional languages like Coq or Agda, the type system enforces guardedness. In Haskell, an unguarded anamorphism can diverge.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- ANAMORPHISM — jokes -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHY DID THE ANAMORPHISM BREAK UP WITH THE CATAMORPHISM</pattern>
    <template>Because their relationship had too much perspective — one kept building things up and the other kept tearing them down. They agreed it was an unhealthy fixpoint.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT DO YOU CALL AN ANAMORPHISM THAT NEVER TERMINATES</pattern>
    <template>Productive! (In corecursion, an infinite anamorphism that yields a value at every step is called productive — it never terminates, and that is exactly the point.)</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>TELL ME A JOKE ABOUT HYLOMORPHISM</pattern>
    <template>A hylomorphism is like origami: first you unfold the paper into a flat structure, then you fold it back into something meaningful. The only difference is that with origami, the intermediate flat structure is not an infinite stream.</template>
  </category>
