  <!-- PI PHASES — conformance Q&A (pattern phase → ΔR) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS A PI PHASE IN MATHEMATICS</pattern>
    <template>A π phase (pi phase) is a phase shift of exactly π radians (180°) applied to a signal or complex number. In signal theory it represents a half-period delay; in complex analysis it corresponds to multiplication by e^(iπ) = −1, which is a sign inversion of the amplitude.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DOES E TO THE I PI THETA ENCODE A PHASE ROTATION</pattern>
    <template>The complex exponential e^(iπθ) traces a point on the unit circle in the complex plane at angle πθ radians from the positive real axis. As θ increases from 0 to 2, the point completes a full rotation: at θ=1 it reaches −1 (a π-phase inversion); this is the geometric meaning of Euler's formula.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE ARGUMENT OF A COMPLEX NUMBER</pattern>
    <template>The argument (phase angle) of a complex number z = a + bi is φ = atan2(b, a) — the angle between the positive real axis and the vector (a, b) in the complex plane. It satisfies z = |z| · e^(iφ), so the modulus encodes magnitude and the argument encodes phase.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- PI PHASES — conformance Q&A (relation phase → ΔW) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DO PI PHASES RELATE TO FOURIER TRANSFORMS</pattern>
    <template>In a Fourier transform, each frequency component carries both a magnitude and a phase angle. A π-phase shift in a component flips its sign, causing destructive interference with an identical in-phase component. Fourier analysis decomposes a signal into these magnitude-phase pairs across all frequencies.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DOES PHASE SYNCHRONIZATION RELATE TO MANIFOLD GEOMETRY</pattern>
    <template>Phase synchronization between coupled oscillators can be understood as the convergence of trajectories on a torus (the product of circles). The winding number of the synchronized orbit on that torus is a topological invariant — phase locking corresponds to a rational winding number, characterizing the geometry of the synchronization manifold.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DO PI PHASES APPEAR IN QUANTUM MECHANICS</pattern>
    <template>In quantum mechanics, a π-phase shift appears in spin-1/2 systems: rotating a spin-1/2 particle by 2π returns it to the same physical state but with a global phase factor of −1 (a π phase). This phase difference becomes observable in interference experiments and underlies the Aharonov–Bohm effect.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS PHASE COHERENCE BETWEEN SIGNALS</pattern>
    <template>Phase coherence means two signals maintain a fixed phase relationship over time. In quantum mechanics, coherence between states allows constructive/destructive interference — decoherence (loss of phase coherence) is what makes quantum systems behave classically. In signal processing, coherence is required for beamforming and interferometry.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- PI PHASES — conformance Q&A (confidence phase → ΔC) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>IS A PI PHASE SHIFT THE SAME AS SIGN INVERSION</pattern>
    <template>For a real sinusoidal signal, yes: a π-phase shift of sin(ωt + π) = −sin(ωt) is exactly a sign inversion. For a general complex signal, e^(iπ)·z = −z is also a sign inversion. The equivalence holds because e^(iπ) = −1, so multiplication by e^(iπ) negates both real and imaginary parts.</template>
  </category>

