  <!-- FOLD — conformance Q&A (pattern phase → ΔR) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS FOLD AS A HIGHER ORDER FUNCTION IN FUNCTIONAL PROGRAMMING</pattern>
    <template>Fold (also called reduce or catamorphism) is a higher-order function that reduces a recursive data structure to a single value by applying a combining function to each element together with an accumulator. It is the canonical way to consume a list: foldr f z [a,b,c] = f a (f b (f c z)).</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN RIGHT FOLD AND LEFT FOLD</pattern>
    <template>Right fold (foldr) associates to the right: f a (f b (f c z)), consuming from the tail first, and is lazy — it can short-circuit on infinite lists. Left fold (foldl) associates to the left: f (f (f z a) b) c, consuming from the head first, and builds a thunk chain that causes stack overflow unless forced strictly (foldl').</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DOES FOLD IMPLEMENT SUM AND PRODUCT AND LIST REVERSAL</pattern>
    <template>Sum: foldl (+) 0 xs — accumulates the running total. Product: foldl (*) 1 xs — accumulates the running product. Reversal: foldl (flip (:)) [] xs — prepends each element to the accumulator, reversing order. All three are specializations of the same fold pattern with different combining functions and zero values.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS A SCAN AND HOW DOES IT DIFFER FROM FOLD</pattern>
    <template>A scan (scanl/scanr) is like fold but it returns all intermediate accumulator values instead of just the final result: scanl f z [a,b,c] = [z, f z a, f (f z a) b, ...]. It is useful for running totals, prefix sums, and anywhere you need the history of a fold, not just its final answer.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- FOLD — conformance Q&A (relation phase → ΔW) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW IS FOLD A CATAMORPHISM OVER A LIST</pattern>
    <template>The list type is the initial algebra of the functor F X = 1 + A × X (empty list or head-cons-tail). A catamorphism over this algebra replaces the Nil constructor with a zero value z and the Cons constructor with the combining function f — that is exactly foldr f z. Any fold is a catamorphism for the appropriate F-algebra.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW ARE FOLD AND UNFOLD DUAL TO EACH OTHER</pattern>
    <template>Catamorphism (fold) is a morphism from the initial algebra of a functor F; anamorphism (unfold) is a morphism to the terminal coalgebra of F. They are dual by reversing the direction of all arrows in their universal properties. Every recursive algorithm can be decomposed into one or both.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- FOLD — conformance Q&A (confidence phase → ΔC) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>IS FOLDL ALWAYS STRICT AND FOLDR ALWAYS LAZY IN HASKELL</pattern>
    <template>No — foldl is lazy by default (it builds a thunk, not a strict accumulation); foldl' from Data.List is the strict version that forces evaluation at each step. foldr is lazy and can handle infinite lists when the combining function is non-strict in its second argument, like (&&) or (:).</template>
  </category>

  <!-- FOLD — jokes -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHY DID FOLDL BREAK UP WITH FOLDR</pattern>
    <template>Because foldr was always building things up from the right and foldl was accumulating grievances from the left — and in the end the whole relationship was just one big unevaluated thunk that blew the stack.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT DO YOU CALL A FOLD THAT NEVER FINISHES</pattern>
    <template>A fold-out — it keeps unfolding and never commits to a final value. (Also known as an infinite corecursive stream when it is well-behaved, and a stack overflow when it is not.)</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHY IS FOLD THE MOST PHILOSOPHICAL HIGHER ORDER FUNCTION</pattern>
    <template>Because it looks at every element of the universe, reduces everything to a single value, and then asks: was this accumulator worth it? Fold is the catamorphism of meaning itself.</template>
  </category>
